.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt TASAMP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tasamp
.Nd Texas Instruments TAS2764 and TAS2770 speaker amplifiers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "tasamp* at iic?"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports the Texas Instruments TAS2764 and TAS2770 mono class-D
amplifiers with speaker voltage and current sense, as found with small
speakers in laptops and phones.
It is a digital audio interface device for a machine driver such as
.Xr applemacaudio 4 ,
not an audio device of its own.
.Pp
These parts protect nothing by themselves: out of reset their output
level is at or near its maximum, and a full-scale input then destroys a
small speaker in seconds.
The driver does not trust whatever feeds it to keep the level down:
.Bl -bullet
.It
The amplifier stays in software shutdown, muted and at its lowest
output level, until the machine driver sets a limit.
There is no default limit, since the driver cannot know the speaker.
.It
The output level is never set above the limit's cap.
While the limit is locked the volume is held at mute, and on the TAS2764
its safe mode is set as well.
.It
Every register access that affects the level is checked: reads are done
twice and compared, writes are read back.
Any I2C failure or mismatch shuts the amplifier down, first in software
and then by pulling its shutdown line low, and it stays down until the
system is restarted.
The line is often shared by all amplifiers of a machine, so one fault
silences them all.
.It
Going quieter, the volume is muted before anything else changes;
going louder, it is raised last.
.El
.Pp
The sense data is transmitted in the time slots given by the device tree
properties
.Dq ti,vmon-slot-no
and
.Dq ti,imon-slot-no ,
counted in the amplifier's 8-bit transmit slots.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr applemacaudio 4 ,
.Xr iic 4 ,
.Xr ssmamp 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Nx 12.0 .
.Sh CAVEATS
The Apple variant SN012776 of the TAS2764 is recognised but kept in
shutdown: it needs writes to undocumented registers whose purpose is
not known.
The TAS5770L, Apple's variant of the TAS2770, is run as a TAS2770; that
it needs nothing more rests on the experience of the Linux maintainers,
not on a data sheet.
